 "why_hela": "it is the one cell line for which the abundance table, the absolute immunoblot counts, the total protein count, the ribosome count and the cell volume have all been measured, so an inventory can be assembled without mixing cell types.",
 "protein_abundance": {
  "table_tests": {
   "order_matters": "T3, the equimolar-spread test, was written first and on its own selects the WRONG table: a log-compressed table has no spread and passes it perfectly. T1, the dynamic-range test, is what makes T3 meaningful.",
   "T1": "real protein abundances in one cell span 4 to 6 orders of magnitude. Pass requires >= 1e4-fold.",
   "T2": "ribosomal proteins outnumber Pol II subunits in a growing cell. Pass requires a median ratio between 3 and 100.",
   "T3": "for an equimolar complex the subunits must come out level. Reported as an accuracy measure, not a pass/fail, because of the order problem above.",
